regulator: rpi-panel-attiny: retry the firmware revision read

attiny_i2c_probe() reads REG_ID exactly once and returns -ENODEV if the value
is not a known revision. -ENODEV is permanent, so the probe is never retried.

The panel's MCU may still be starting at that point. On a BananaPi M2 Magic the
first read returns 0x8b, while a moment later it returns the correct 0xc3. This
only began failing after an unrelated boot-time change (adding "quiet" to the
kernel cmdline) moved this probe from ~1.5 s to ~0.5 s after power-on -- the
hardware dependency was always there, merely hidden by a slow boot.

The consequence is disproportionate: this MCU supplies power, backlight and
reset to the panel, so its failure leaves the DSI panel unattached, the
connector "disconnected", and the display dark:

  rpi_touchscreen_attiny 0-0045: Unknown Atmel firmware revision: 0x8b
  mipi-dsi 1ca0000.dsi.0: deferred probe pending: supplier 0-0045 not ready

Retry the read for about a second before giving up.

Signed-off-by: RetroBPI_M2M

--- a/drivers/regulator/rpi-panel-attiny-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/rpi-panel-attiny-regulator.c
@@ -32,6 +32,10 @@
 #define REG_WRITE_DATA_H	0x90
 #define REG_WRITE_DATA_L	0x91
 
+/* How long to wait for the panel MCU to come up before giving up. */
+#define ATTINY_ID_RETRIES	20
+#define ATTINY_ID_RETRY_MS	50
+
 #define PA_LCD_DITHB		BIT(0)
 #define PA_LCD_MODE		BIT(1)
 #define PA_LCD_LR		BIT(2)
@@ -281,6 +285,7 @@
 	struct attiny_lcd *state;
 	struct regmap *regmap;
 	unsigned int data;
+	unsigned int attempt;
 	int ret;
 
 	state = devm_kzalloc(&i2c->dev, sizeof(*state), GFP_KERNEL);
@@ -300,8 +305,27 @@
 			ret);
 		return ret;
 	}
+	/*
+	 * The panel's MCU may still be starting when we get here. On a fast boot
+	 * this probe runs ~0.5 s after power-on and the first REG_ID read comes
+	 * back as garbage (0x8b seen on a BananaPi M2 Magic), while a moment
+	 * later it reads the correct 0xc3.
+	 *
+	 * Reading once and returning -ENODEV is fatal, and it takes the display
+	 * with it: this MCU supplies power, backlight and reset to the panel, so
+	 * the DSI panel never attaches and the connector reports disconnected.
+	 * Retry for about a second before giving up.
+	 */
+	for (attempt = 0; attempt < ATTINY_ID_RETRIES; attempt++) {
+		ret = attiny_i2c_read(i2c, REG_ID, &data);
+		if (ret >= 0 && (data == 0xde || data == 0xc3))
+			break;
+		msleep(ATTINY_ID_RETRY_MS);
+	}
+
+	if (attempt)
+		dev_info(&i2c->dev, "panel MCU ready after %d retries\n", attempt);
 
-	ret = attiny_i2c_read(i2c, REG_ID, &data);
 	if (ret < 0) {
 		dev_err(&i2c->dev, "Failed to read REG_ID reg: %d\n", ret);
 		return ret;
